sec.p:
An 18‐year‐old male, with history of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) since the age of 15 (Figure

1A
), was diagnosed with stage 4 neuroblastoma (Figure

1B
). The echocardiography shown in Figure

1A
 was performed 1 month before the inmunotherapy. The patient had primary refractory bone disease after induction chemotherapy (adapted protocol N7 from the Memorial Sloan Kettering Center in New York with cyclophosphamide/doxorubicin/vincristine/cisplatin/etoposide) and was treated with naxitamab and GM‐CSF immunotherapy. Immediately after completing the first cycle (three doses of naxitamab at 3 mg/kg/dose and 10 days of SC GM‐CSF) presented with chest pain and increased troponin levels (peak of 0.335 ng/mL, normal value <0.200 ng/mL, 10 days after last naxitamab infusion). EKG had sinus tachycardia with T‐wave polarity changes in left precordial leads (Figure

1C
) with respect to the baseline EKG (Figure

1D
). Echocardiogram showed significant increase in septal hypertrophy (24 mm → Z + 9.2 compared with previous interventricular septum size of 15 mm) (Figure

1E
), with a peak dynamic gradient in the left ventricular outflow tract (LVOT) of 80 mmHg (Figure

1F
), which was not present on previous scans. Treatment with beta‐blocker and corticosteroids was started given the suspicion of myocarditis. Given symptoms of inappropriate sinus tachycardia and orthostatic hypotension, it was decided to start ivabradine. Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) was performed (7 days after the onset of symptoms) and showed mild signs of myocardial oedema (Figure

1G
), as well as an increase in myocardial T1 mapping and increased extracellular volume of up to 30% (Figure

1H
). There was heterogeneous intramyocardial late gadolinium enhancement at the septal level (Figure

1I
). Progressive clinical improvement, normalization of troponins, and normalization of septal hypertrophy after initiation of treatment was noticed after 3 weeks (Figure

1J
).

A 15‐year‐old male was diagnosed with osteosarcoma in proximal humerus and treated with the usual chemotherapy protocol (SEHOP‐2010) until December 2020. He presented lung metastatic relapse of osteosarcoma with GD2 expression in June 2021. Firstly, he received two cycles of gemcitabine–docetaxel without objective response and after that was treated with naxitamab and G‐CSF immunotherapy. On the eighth day of the infusion, an echocardiogram was performed due to symptoms of chest pain, tachycardia, and orthostatic hypotension. Echocardiogram showed global myocardial hypertrophy (Figure

2A
) and a significant increase in septal myocardial diastolic diameters (20 mm → Z + 6.2 compared with previous interventricular septum size of 10 mm) without gradient in the LVOT. Troponin I figures were normal at all times; serial levels D + 8, D + 9, D + 11, D + 14, D + 16 (maximum level of 0.028 ng/mL) were performed. EKG had sinus tachycardia (Figure

2B
). Treatment with beta‐blocker and corticosteroids was started given the suspicion of myocarditis. Ivabradine was added to treatment due to inappropriate sinus tachycardia. Cardiac MRI was performed (8 days after the onset of symptoms) and showed septal hypertrophy (Figure

2C
) and mild signs of myocardial oedema (Figure

2D
), as well as an increase in myocardial T1 mapping (Figure

2E
) with minimal intramyocardial late enhancement at the septal level (Figure

2F
). Progressive clinical improvement and normalization of septal hypertrophy after initiation of treatment was noticed after 3 weeks (Figure

2G
).
